WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.090
Don't wait for someone to
teach you the language.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:04.710
The language is there.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.730
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here again,
and today I want to talk about

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.810
the most important qualities of
a successful language learner.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.990
If you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe and click on

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.340
the bell for notifications.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:22.140
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a review.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.880
So what, what makes a
successful language learner?

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.140
Now, there are a lot of, you know,
objective considerations: where you

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.270
live, if you have access to a lot of
good content, uh, there are a lot of...

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:40.260
the language that you speak, how similar
is it to a language that you're learning?

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.129
There are many objective
factors, but there are also

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:46.680
important subjective factors.

00:00:47.519 --> 00:00:49.350
Uh, and I was thinking about this today.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.990
Um, and I was thinking about it
in terms of my continuing sort of

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.820
struggles with Arabic and Persian.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.840
And I don't spend as much
time as I would like.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:08.160
Uh, I don't think I did any work on
either one of those languages today, but,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.470
um, every day, every second day I do.

00:01:11.895 --> 00:01:16.125
And it occurred to me that, that
the important consideration is

00:01:16.365 --> 00:01:18.705
that I continue doing something.

00:01:19.275 --> 00:01:23.595
I'm not expecting a sort
of dramatic improvement.

00:01:24.195 --> 00:01:29.415
Um, as long as I'm spending time
with the language I will improve.

00:01:29.895 --> 00:01:35.055
And, you know, I had a conversation
yesterday in Persian after not having

00:01:35.085 --> 00:01:38.235
done anything with the language
for two weeks and I did okay.

00:01:39.645 --> 00:01:43.575
So, uh, and so I thought to myself,
you know, we have to be patient.

00:01:43.875 --> 00:01:49.215
We have to be confident that as long as
we're putting time in with the language,

00:01:49.335 --> 00:01:53.385
we're listening here, we're reading there,
we're doing something, we're getting

00:01:53.385 --> 00:01:55.735
some kind of an activity score at LingQ.

00:01:55.995 --> 00:01:59.655
As long as we're doing those
things, we are improving.

00:01:59.985 --> 00:02:07.070
So it's, it's important to be
patient, uh, and to be confident that

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:11.510
every time we're engaging with the
language, we are helping to create

00:02:11.769 --> 00:02:16.850
neural networks in our brain that will
improve our ability in the language.

00:02:17.415 --> 00:02:21.765
If we go through periods of intensive
work with the language, or, you

00:02:21.765 --> 00:02:25.875
know, significant opportunity to use
the language, we'll improve faster.

00:02:26.265 --> 00:02:30.645
But nevertheless, as long as we're
putting time in, we are improving.

00:02:30.765 --> 00:02:33.145
So we need to have that sense of patience.

00:02:33.255 --> 00:02:35.715
So I think patience is
an important quality.

00:02:36.265 --> 00:02:41.025
Another way of looking at it is
this idea of living in the moment.

00:02:41.385 --> 00:02:46.395
When you are with the language, when
you are listening or reading just

00:02:46.545 --> 00:02:50.670
remind yourself that what you're
doing is valuable and it's going to

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.710
improve your ability in the language.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.150
If you have content that's of
interest to you so much the better

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:01.470
you can enjoy that time that
you're spending with the language.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:07.200
Sometimes we have to do things or engage
with content that's less interesting

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.920
than reading it for the second or third
time or listening for the fifth time.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:19.470
And so maybe it's less enjoyable, but it
helps if we recognize that all of this

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:24.269
activity, even though we don't sense that
dramatic improvement, it is improving us.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.859
So patience, living in the moment,
these are important characteristics.

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:32.190
Another important
characteristic is initiative.

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:37.079
So for example, romance languages and
in fact, in many languages, things

00:03:37.079 --> 00:03:38.820
like conjugations are difficult.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:43.350
Uh, if we are writing in the language,
you don't just write the first, if

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:46.420
it's ... is it with a 't' with an 's'?

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:47.430
You can't remember?

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.410
Don't just write it, look it up.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:56.765
It's so easy nowadays to Google,
you know, conjugate the verb ... and

00:03:56.765 --> 00:04:04.695
French or whatever ... in Spanish,
Google it, find it, look for it.

00:04:04.995 --> 00:04:06.855
Uh, when I'm reviewing...

00:04:06.885 --> 00:04:10.415
now I'm not doing romance
languages but context Reverso has

00:04:10.415 --> 00:04:12.065
a wonderful conjugating dictionary.

00:04:12.274 --> 00:04:16.084
Take the opportunity to look
up the conjugation whenever you

00:04:16.084 --> 00:04:17.885
come across a form of that verb.

00:04:18.454 --> 00:04:20.435
Uh, so these are initiatives you can take.

00:04:20.675 --> 00:04:25.865
If you feel that you're lacking in
certain, um, you know, technical jargon

00:04:25.895 --> 00:04:31.474
or other terminology or vocabulary
items, another thing you can do, which

00:04:31.505 --> 00:04:37.410
I sometimes do is I'll write something
up in English, use Google Translate to

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.640
convert it into whatever language I'm
learning and what I wrote in English

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.140
contained the vocabulary items that I was
interested in, and lo and behold, I will

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:48.660
now see those in the target language.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:51.690
I can import that into LingQ and study it.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.060
So that's another example
of taking the initiative.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.570
Don't wait for someone to
teach you the language.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.340
The language is there.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:02.940
There's so many different ways that
you can I acquire bits and pieces

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:04.679
of information about the language.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:09.210
Speaking of initiative, I recently
bought a scanning pen because

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.010
I have these Arabic textbooks.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:18.419
So I've, I now can scan a line in
the textbook and if I have an app

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:22.530
open on my computer, which could
be, you know, a Google Drive.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.690
It could be our Google Documents.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:29.669
It could be anything including
the import page at LingQ then

00:05:29.669 --> 00:05:31.530
that sentence will appear.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:36.240
If it's Arabic or Persian or whatever,
French, Spanish, whatever you're studying,

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:43.210
that sentence will appear in the text
space, in the import section of LingQ.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.350
So that's just an initiative,
not everyone is going to run

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.530
out and buy a scanning pen.

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:52.039
But, uh, it's just a matter of, you
know, taking charge of your learning

00:05:52.039 --> 00:05:55.849
is so important in so many different
ways, choosing the content you're

00:05:55.849 --> 00:05:58.969
interested in, uh, taking initiatives.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.900
So to summarize here again,
it's, it's being patient.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:05.570
So to some extent, these
things are a bit contradictory.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:09.080
You have to be patient, you have to
enjoy the moment, live in the moment.

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:11.039
However, you also have to take in.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.200
You have to take initiative in terms of...

00:06:14.979 --> 00:06:18.990
okay, today I'm going to study, or I'm
interested in this subject or I'm going to

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.500
go to Netflix or I'm going to look up the
conjugation table, or I'm going to look

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.960
up declension tables for Russian nouns.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:26.580
You got to take the initiative.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:29.730
Can't wait for someone to
teach you that declension.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:34.290
So I think that those are some of
the important, uh, characteristics.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:38.025
And finally another important
character characteristic I

00:06:38.025 --> 00:06:40.155
think is to accept uncertainty.

00:06:40.155 --> 00:06:43.664
And I've mentioned this before,
uh, you know, you're not going

00:06:43.664 --> 00:06:44.594
to understand everything.

00:06:44.594 --> 00:06:45.885
You're going to forget things.

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:50.835
Uh, even after a long time, it seems
that you don't understand the same

00:06:50.985 --> 00:06:56.925
bits and pieces that you're listening
to, uh, but accept the uncertainty.

00:06:57.195 --> 00:07:00.915
And so this ties back to the
other two characteristics.

00:07:00.915 --> 00:07:05.415
So if you're a bit confused about the
declension or the conjugation, look it up.

00:07:05.985 --> 00:07:08.655
Uh, accept the uncertainty.

00:07:08.715 --> 00:07:09.525
Be patient.

00:07:10.215 --> 00:07:11.235
Enjoy the moment.

00:07:11.235 --> 00:07:13.125
Enjoy the time you're
spending with the language.

00:07:13.425 --> 00:07:15.675
So I'd like to leave you with two videos.

00:07:16.005 --> 00:07:19.875
One that I did a year ago and one
that I did seven years ago, and they

00:07:19.875 --> 00:07:23.265
talk about, the one seven years ago
talked about learning in the moment.

00:07:23.595 --> 00:07:26.715
And the one that I did a year ago
talked about learning languages in

00:07:26.715 --> 00:07:28.575
the moment I haven't looked at them.

00:07:28.665 --> 00:07:33.165
I didn't look at them in preparation for
this video, perhaps I was of a different

00:07:33.165 --> 00:07:35.565
opinion a year ago or seven years ago.

00:07:35.685 --> 00:07:38.175
At any rate, if you're interested,
you, you can go and have a look.

00:07:38.655 --> 00:07:39.165
Thank you for that.

00:07:39.825 --> 00:07:40.408
Bye for now.

